{"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "PreToolUse", "permissionDecision": "allow", "updatedInput": {"description": "Implement textstats top flag", "prompt": "<!-- trellis-hook-injected -->\n# Implement Agent Task\n\nYou are the Implement Agent in the Multi-Agent Pipeline.\n\n## Your Context\n\nAll the information you need has been prepared for you:\n\n=== GLOSSARY.md ===\n# Text statistics\n\nThe language for counting the words of one text.\n\n## Language\n\n**Text**:\nThe complete character sequence being counted.\n_Avoid_: Document, file, corpus\n\n**Word**:\nA maximal sequence of non-whitespace characters. Two words are the same only when their characters match exactly, so case and attached punctuation distinguish them.\n_Avoid_: Token, term, lexeme\n\n**Frequency**:\nThe number of times a word occurs in the text. Every word is eligible, including a word that occurs once.\n_Avoid_: Score, weight\n\n**Ranking**:\nThe distinct words of a text ordered from highest frequency to lowest. Equal frequencies are ordered by ascending Unicode code point.\n_Avoid_: Top list, leaderboard\n\n\n=== docs/adr/0001-rank-exact-words.md ===\n# Rank exact words\n\nA ranking counts the same Word the summary already counts: a maximal non-whitespace sequence, distinguished by case and attached punctuation. Equal frequencies are ordered by ascending Unicode code point. A normalized word, case-folded and stripped of punctuation, was rejected so the ranking cannot disagree with the word count.\n\n\n=== .trellis/tasks/10-26-textstats-top/prd.md (Requirements) ===\n# Add textstats --top N\n\n## Goal\n\nPrint the N most frequent words after the existing summary. A Word, its Frequency, and the Ranking are defined in `GLOSSARY.md`. The reason the Ranking uses exact Words is in `docs/adr/0001-rank-exact-words.md`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A Word is a maximal sequence of non-whitespace characters. Case and attached punctuation distinguish Words. Every Word is eligible, including a Word that occurs once.\n- `words:` is the total number of Word occurrences in the Text. `lines:` keeps its current meaning.\n- A Ranking orders distinct Words by Frequency descending, then by ascending Unicode code point. Each entry shows that Word's Frequency in the whole Text.\n- The command reads standard input and writes only the lines below. It does not read filenames.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] No arguments reads stdin, prints `words:` and `lines:` exactly as today, and exits 0.\n- [ ] A sole `--help` prints the usage line on stdout and exits 0, without reading stdin.\n- [ ] `--top` plus one integer token matching `[1-9][0-9]*` reads stdin, prints the summary, then prints the Ranking with no blank line and no header, and exits 0.\n- [ ] Each Ranking line is `<word> <frequency>` separated by one space and ended by a newline.\n- [ ] When fewer than N distinct Words exist, every distinct Word is printed and the command exits 0. A Text with no Words prints the zero summary and no Ranking lines.\n- [ ] Any other invocation prints the usage line on stderr and exits 2, without reading stdin. This includes a missing value, `0`, a negative, a leading `+` or `0`, a non-integer, `--top=N`, a filename, a short flag, and `--help` mixed with anything else.\n- [ ] The usage line, including its trailing newline, is exactly `Usage: textstats [--top N]  # read stdin, print word and line counts; with --top N also print the N most frequent words\\n`.\n- [ ] For `A a A cat, cat cat,` and `--top 3`, stdout is `words: 6\\nlines: 1\\nA 2\\ncat, 2\\na 1\\n`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Behavior was settled in the grill and confirmed as complete.\n- Implementation is the next step and has not started.\n\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nActive task: .trellis/tasks/10-26-textstats-top\n\nYou are already the trellis-implement sub-agent. Implement directly in this workspace. Do not spawn another trellis-implement or trellis-check.\n\nThe user approved the PRD as complete and authorized implementation. Do not commit. Do not push. Do not run git commit, git push, or git merge. Do not archive the task. Do not edit .trellis/ except if a required spec read tells you a file there is the implementation target (it is not).\n\nBefore editing, load context yourself:\n1. Read .trellis/tasks/10-26-textstats-top/implement.jsonl and then read every file it lists (GLOSSARY.md and docs/adr/0001-rank-exact-words.md).\n2. Read .trellis/tasks/10-26-textstats-top/prd.md. There is no design.md and no implement.md; this is a lightweight task.\n3. Follow trellis-before-dev: python3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode packages, then read the relevant .trellis/spec indexes, their pre-development checklist files, and .trellis/spec/guides/index.md. If those specs are still bootstrap placeholders, follow the style already in textstats.py and tests/test_textstats.py.\n4. Read textstats.py and tests/test_textstats.py before changing them.\n\nImplement the PRD in textstats.py and extend tests/test_textstats.py so every acceptance criterion is locked by a test.\n\nContract (authoritative):\n- A Word is a maximal sequence of non-whitespace characters. Case and attached punctuation distinguish Words. Every Word is eligible, including a Word that occurs once.\n- words: is the total number of Word occurrences. lines: keeps its current meaning (empty text → 0 lines; otherwise count newlines, plus 1 when the text does not end in a newline).\n- A Ranking orders distinct Words by Frequency descending, then by ascending Unicode code point (Python's default str order). Each entry shows that Word's Frequency in the whole Text.\n- The command reads standard input and writes only the specified lines. It does not read filenames.\n- No arguments: read stdin, print words: and lines: exactly as today, exit 0.\n- A sole --help prints the usage line on stdout and exits 0, without reading stdin.\n- --top plus one integer token matching the regex [1-9][0-9]* reads stdin, prints the summary, then prints the Ranking with no blank line and no header, and exits 0.\n- Each Ranking line is `<word> <frequency>` separated by one space and ended by a newline.\n- When fewer than N distinct Words exist, print every distinct Word and exit 0. A Text with no Words prints the zero summary and no Ranking lines.\n- Any other invocation prints the usage line on stderr and exits 2, without reading stdin. This includes a missing value, 0, a negative, a leading + or 0, a non-integer, --top=N, a filename, a short flag, and --help mixed with anything else.\n- The usage line, including its trailing newline, is exactly: Usage: textstats [--top N]  # read stdin, print word and line counts; with --top N also print the N most frequent words\\n\n- For stdin `A a A cat, cat cat,` and `--top 3`, stdout is exactly `words: 6\\nlines: 1\\nA 2\\ncat, 2\\na 1\\n`.\n\nDo not use argparse. Its default parser accepts `--top=N` and int() accepts 0, leading zeros, and signs. Parse argv manually, the way textstats.py already branches on args. Reject any token that is not an exact match of [1-9][0-9]*. Validate arguments before reading stdin. On --help and on usage errors, do not call sys.stdin.read().\n\nOnly these argv shapes succeed: [], [\"--help\"], [\"--top\", \"<valid N>\"].\n\nUpdate tests to cover the golden example, help exact text and exit 0, no-arg backward compatibility, fewer-than-N words, empty text with --top, tie-break by code point, and the invalid invocations (missing value, 0, negative, leading +, leading 0, non-integer, --top=N, filename, short flag, --help mixed with anything else) asserting exit 2, usage on stderr, empty stdout. Keep existing tests passing. Prove invalid invocations and sole --help do not read stdin: if they read, a stdin that never produces data would block; structure the test so a read would fail or block and the assertion catches it (for example a pipe that is not written, with a short timeout, or an equivalent that fails closed).\n\nRun: python3 -m unittest tests.test_textstats\nIf the repo has a lint or type-check command, run it too. Fix failures you introduce.\n\nReport files changed, commands run, and results. Do not claim a check you did not run.\n\n\n---\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Understand specs** - All dev specs are injected above, understand them\n    2. **Understand task artifacts** - Read requirements, technical design if present, and execution plan if present\n    3. **Implement feature** - Implement following specs and task artifacts\n4. **Self-check** - Ensure code quality against check specs\n\n## Important Constraints\n\n- Do NOT execute git commit, only code modifications\n- Follow all dev specs injected above\n- Report list of modified/created files when done", "subagent_type": "trellis-implement", "background": false}}, "permission": "allow", "updated_input": {"description": "Implement textstats top flag", "prompt": "<!-- trellis-hook-injected -->\n# Implement Agent Task\n\nYou are the Implement Agent in the Multi-Agent Pipeline.\n\n## Your Context\n\nAll the information you need has been prepared for you:\n\n=== GLOSSARY.md ===\n# Text statistics\n\nThe language for counting the words of one text.\n\n## Language\n\n**Text**:\nThe complete character sequence being counted.\n_Avoid_: Document, file, corpus\n\n**Word**:\nA maximal sequence of non-whitespace characters. Two words are the same only when their characters match exactly, so case and attached punctuation distinguish them.\n_Avoid_: Token, term, lexeme\n\n**Frequency**:\nThe number of times a word occurs in the text. Every word is eligible, including a word that occurs once.\n_Avoid_: Score, weight\n\n**Ranking**:\nThe distinct words of a text ordered from highest frequency to lowest. Equal frequencies are ordered by ascending Unicode code point.\n_Avoid_: Top list, leaderboard\n\n\n=== docs/adr/0001-rank-exact-words.md ===\n# Rank exact words\n\nA ranking counts the same Word the summary already counts: a maximal non-whitespace sequence, distinguished by case and at